HTML_CONTENT = """<section class="td-analysis">
  <div class="td-analysis-inner">
    <h2>FENCING CONTRACTOR MARKETING: HOW TO WIN MORE FENCE JOBS IN 2025</h2>
    <p class="td-answer">Fencing contractors who consistently win new jobs aren't the ones with the lowest prices — they're the ones who show up first online, look credible when homeowners check them out, and make it easy to request a quote. In competitive Midwest markets like the Quad Cities and Peoria, a strong Google Business Profile, targeted local SEO, and a clean portfolio website are the three things separating busy fencing companies from ones chasing leads. If your digital presence isn't generating consistent inbound calls, this is where to start.</p>

    <h2>WHAT MAKES FENCING LEADS DIFFERENT</h2>
    <p>Fencing projects sit in a sweet spot for contractors: jobs are large enough to be worth fighting for — residential installs typically run $2,500 to $8,000, commercial projects higher — but homeowners still comparison-shop heavily before committing. The average fencing buyer gets two or three quotes, spends real time looking at photos of finished work, and makes a highly visual decision based on what they think the fence will look like in their yard. That means your marketing has to do two things at once: get you found, and then visually convince the prospect you're the right crew before they ever pick up the phone.</p>
    <p>Local competition in fencing is real but beatable. Most markets have a handful of dominant companies that have been around for decades and a long tail of newer operations without much online presence. The deciding factors for most buyers come down to three things: how fast you respond to an inquiry, how your finished work looks in photos, and what past customers say about you in reviews. Price matters, but it's rarely the only driver — especially for homeowners investing in cedar privacy fencing or ornamental iron for curb appeal. In markets like Peoria or the Quad Cities, being the most visible and most trusted fence company online is a more reliable growth strategy than being the cheapest one.</p>

    <h2>MARKETING CHANNELS THAT ACTUALLY WORK FOR FENCE COMPANIES</h2>
    <h3>Local SEO and Google Business Profile</h3>
    <p>When someone in Moline or Pekin types "fence company near me" or "wood fence installer Peoria IL," Google surfaces a map pack of three local businesses before any organic website results. Getting into that map pack is the single highest-leverage move a fencing contractor can make online. It requires a fully optimized and actively maintained Google Business Profile — complete with your service categories, service area cities, hours, and most importantly, a steady stream of project photos and customer reviews. Homeowners searching for fencing are almost always in buying mode, not just browsing. Showing up in that map pack with 80+ reviews and a gallery of recent installs converts at a dramatically higher rate than anything else at this budget level.</p>
    <h3>Google Local Services Ads</h3>
    <p>Google Local Services Ads (LSAs) display above everything else — above traditional Google Ads, above the map pack, above organic results. For fencing contractors, they work especially well because the "Google Guaranteed" badge signals instant credibility to homeowners who don't know you yet. More importantly, you pay only when a verified lead actually calls or messages you through the ad — not per click, per impression, or per bounce. In a market where a single booked fence job can return $3,000 to $10,000, paying $30 to $80 per qualified lead is a favorable trade. LSAs also reward businesses with strong reviews, so the effort you put into reputation management directly improves your LSA performance.</p>
    <h3>Before-and-After Photo Galleries</h3>
    <p>Fencing is one of the most visual home improvement categories that exists. A homeowner deciding between wood privacy, white vinyl, or black chain-link is largely making that decision by looking at pictures of finished jobs that look like their house. Before-and-after photos — ideally geotagged and organized by fence type — are not a nice-to-have for a fencing website, they're the core conversion asset. Every install your crew completes is a marketing opportunity. A quick set of photos taken at job completion, uploaded to your website gallery and Google Business Profile, builds a visual proof library that compounds over time. Fence companies with 50+ project photos in their gallery close at higher rates than companies with a cleaner site and no portfolio.</p>
    <h3>Reputation and Reviews</h3>
    <p>Reviews drive fencing purchase decisions harder than almost any other trade category, because the stakes feel personal — this is someone's yard, their property line, their privacy. Buyers read reviews looking for specific signals: did the crew show up when they said they would, did the finished fence match what was quoted, was cleanup thorough, and did the company stand behind the work if something needed adjusting. Reviews that mention specific fence materials ("cedar privacy fence," "black aluminum ornamental"), specific neighborhoods or cities, and specific outcomes ("looks great after two years, no warping") carry the most weight both with potential customers and with Google's ranking algorithm. A fencing company with 60 Google reviews averaging 4.8 stars will outperform a competitor with 12 reviews at 5.0 in virtually every market.</p>

    <h2>WHAT DOES FENCING MARKETING COST?</h2>
    <table class="td-cost-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Typical Monthly Budget</th><th>Expected Return</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Google Business Profile (organic)</td><td>$0 + setup time</td><td>10–30 leads/mo in competitive markets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local SEO</td><td>$500–$1,500/mo</td><td>Consistent organic lead flow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Google Local Services Ads</td><td>$400–$1,200/mo</td><td>Pay only per verified lead</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Google Ads (Search)</td><td>$800–$2,500/mo</td><td>Immediate visibility, higher CPL</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Website + Content</td><td>$1,200–$3,500 build</td><td>Foundation for all digital channels</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>HOW TO RANK HIGHER FOR FENCING SEARCHES</h2>
    <p>Google ranks fencing contractor websites based on relevance, authority, and geographic signals. Relevance means your site clearly tells Google exactly what types of fencing you install and where. A single generic "fencing services" page does not accomplish this — you need dedicated pages for each material category you offer (wood, vinyl, chain-link, ornamental iron, aluminum, split rail) and dedicated location pages for each city in your service area. Authority comes from reviews, backlinks from local directories and suppliers, and consistent NAP (name, address, phone) data across the web. Geographic signals come from your Google Business Profile, the cities mentioned in your content, and reviews that reference local neighborhoods. The fencing companies ranking at the top of Google in any Midwest market have all three of these working together.</p>
    <ul class="td-note-list">
      <li>Separate pages for wood, vinyl, chain-link, ornamental fencing</li>
      <li>Location pages for each city you serve</li>
      <li>Project gallery with captions and alt text</li>
      <li>Schema markup for local business</li>
      <li>Google Business Profile with photos updated monthly</li>
      <li>Reviews mentioning specific fence types and neighborhoods</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="td-soft-cta">
      <p>Toughjobs works with fencing contractors across the Midwest. <a href="contact.html">Talk to us about your market</a> — we'll tell you what's actually working for fence companies in your area.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS — FENCING CONTRACTOR MARKETING</h2>
    <div class="td-faq-list">
      <div class="td-faq-item">
        <h3>How long does it take for fencing SEO to work?</h3>
        <p>For most fencing contractors starting from a weak online presence, expect three to six months before you see meaningful movement in organic Google rankings. The local map pack tends to move faster — sometimes within six to ten weeks if your Google Business Profile is fully optimized and you're actively collecting reviews. Longer-tail keyword rankings for specific pages (like "vinyl fence installer Peoria IL") typically take four to six months to build momentum. SEO is not an immediate traffic source, which is why we often recommend running Google Local Services Ads in parallel during the first few months — they generate leads while the organic foundation is being built.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="td-faq-item">
        <h3>Should a fencing company use Google Ads or Local Services Ads?</h3>
        <p>They serve different purposes and work best together. Local Services Ads are the higher-trust option — the Google Guaranteed badge matters to homeowners evaluating fence companies, and the pay-per-lead model means you're not burning budget on clicks that don't convert. LSAs are the right starting point if budget is limited. Google Search Ads give you more control — you can bid on specific fence types, target specific cities, and run seasonal promotions around spring installation season. For companies ready to scale lead volume, running both channels simultaneously captures a larger share of the search page and makes your brand appear dominant in the local market. If you can only do one, start with LSAs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="td-faq-item">
        <h3>How important is a website for a fencing contractor?</h3>
        <p>A website is the single most important owned asset in your marketing stack, and for fencing specifically it does things no other channel can. It hosts your photo gallery — which is your primary conversion tool. It lets you build out service pages for every fence material and style you offer, which is how you rank on Google for high-intent searches. It provides a credible destination when homeowners check you out after seeing your Google Business Profile or LSA. And it gives you control over your quote request process. A fencing company relying only on Facebook or a Google Business Profile listing is leaving significant revenue on the table and is one algorithm change away from losing their lead flow entirely.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="td-faq-item">
        <h3>What should a fencing contractor's website include?</h3>
        <p>At minimum: a homepage that clearly states what you install, where you work, and how to contact you; individual service pages for each fence type you offer (wood privacy, vinyl, chain-link, ornamental, aluminum, split rail); a project gallery organized by material or style with real photos from your jobs; a reviews section pulling from Google; a service area page listing every city and suburb you cover; and an online quote request form that's easy to complete on mobile. Optional but high-value additions include a fence cost estimator or FAQs page targeting common questions ("how much does a wood fence cost in Peoria"), which capture early-stage researchers before they're ready to call.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="td-faq-item">
        <h3>How do I compete with big fencing companies online?</h3>
        <p>Local SEO and Google Business Profile are the great equalizers in fencing — a well-reviewed local shop with an active GBP regularly outranks large regional companies with bigger ad budgets, because Google's local algorithm heavily weights proximity, review volume, and relevance. Your advantages as a local contractor are real: you can respond to leads faster, your reviews feel more personal and specific, and you can niche your marketing around materials or styles that larger companies handle generically. Focus your energy on a specific material (cedar fencing, ornamental iron) or a specific geographic pocket, build review volume in that niche, and dominate the local pack in your core service cities before trying to expand. Speed-to-respond is also a legitimate competitive edge — studies consistently show that the first contractor to follow up on a fencing inquiry wins the job at a significantly higher rate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>"""

filepath = os.path.join(TRADE_DIR, "trade-fencing.html")
with open(filepath, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    src = f.read()

if '<!-- SEO-TRADE-CONTENT:START -->' in src:
    print('Already injected')
elif '</main>' not in src:
    print('ERROR: no </main>')
else:
    injection = '<!-- SEO-TRADE-CONTENT:START -->\n' + CSS_BLOCK + '\n' + HTML_CONTENT + '\n<!-- SEO-TRADE-CONTENT:END -->\n'
    new_src = src.replace('</main>', injection + '</main>', 1)
    with open(filepath, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
        f.write(new_src)
    print('OK: trade-fencing.html')
